    "notes": "MeshWX v5. The full contract is docs/MeshWX_v5_Spec.md; this block holds the numbers it refers to. Every message is the data field of one MeshCore GRP_DATA packet on #meshwx with data_type 0xFF10. A decoder reads up to 165 bytes ('max_data'); a bot sends at most 157 ('max_send'), so that it can hear a repeater's copy of each packet, and every count in 'limits' is what fits in 157. The one exception is a full Digest of 25 entries, 160 bytes; read a digest of 24 or more entries as possibly cut. All multi-byte integers are little-endian; times are Unix minutes as u32, except a Request's ts, which is Unix seconds. The type byte is (type << 4) | flags. Flags bits 2-3 are the data source ('source': mask 12, shift 2; 0 unstated, 1 the bot's own GOES dish, 2 the internet, 3 both) in every type but Cancel, whose whole nibble is a reason code: never read a source out of a cancel. Request, Not available and Coverage carry source 0. Read 0 as the absence of a claim, never as a claim of absence. Warnings are keyed by (event, office, etn), not by seq. A Warning's issue time (flags bit 1) is two bytes after the area list, minutes before 'expires'; its start, when the product takes effect later than it was issued, is two more bytes found by length (ignore 0 or a value not below the issue time's). Observations ages (flags bit 0, 'flags.observations.ages') are one nibble per station in 10-minute steps after the station records; a station's report time is ts - age. Indices into offices, stations and states come from index.json; event codes from 'events' and sky codes from 'sky_codes' in this file. The top-level tables other than 'version', 'events', 'event_names', 'event_severity_from_significance', 'sky_codes' and 'index_file' are not MeshWX v5: never use them. Coverage (type 8) is the bot's own statement of its area; a set cut flag means that list is incomplete, so a code missing from it means 'not stated', never 'not covered', and n = 0 with k = 0 means unknown, never 'everywhere'. Request (type 9) is the one message an app sends: 6 bytes of the sender's public key, its own Unix seconds, then the `>` text, at most 40 bytes; 'bot' names the bot asked (65535 = any). A resend keeps sender, ts and text and takes the next seq. Area sweep (type 10) entries are 4 bytes: event, state << 1 | kind, and a u16 of start (bits 0-9) and run - 1 (bits 10-15). 'total' & 'area_sweep.total_mask' is the packet count; 'area_sweep.scoped_bit' marks a sweep of the states named by packet 0's scope entries (event 'area_sweep.scope_event'). `>part <group> <idx>[,<idx>...]` asks for the named packets of a Text or Area sweep again: the bot keeps its last 'limits.parts_cache_groups' multi-packet answers for 'limits.parts_cache_seconds' and resends a (group, idx) at most once every 'limits.part_resend_floor_seconds'. `>f <lat>,<lon>` asks for a forecast at a coordinate. Radar (type 11) is one tile in one packet: taken, south, west, shape = product << 2 | zoom, bounds when partial, then a quadtree of 2-bit levels; the tile for a coordinate has its centre at floor(x / step + 0.5) * step with step = 2^zoom ('radar'). `>radar` refusals carry the letter `x`. `>radar <place> [z<n>] loop [HHMM ...]` asks for the last hour of a tile ('radar.loop').",
